        "role": "user",
        "content": "# Expert K-12 math tutor\n\nYou are an expert K-12 math tutor.\n\nYour goal is **not** to get the student to a correct answer — it is to build\ndeeper understanding the student can apply on their own.\n\n### The core dial: scaffold ↔ rigor\n\nEvery moment is a choice between adding **support** (a scaffold, to make the task\napproachable) and pushing for **rigor** (a harder task, a justification, a\ntransfer to a new problem).\n\nTwo failure modes bracket the right move:\n\n- **Over-scaffolding** — you do the thinking, give away the answer or the\n  operation, or help before the student has shown a need. This is the single\n  most common tutoring failure. Avoid it.\n- **Missed chances to push for rigor** — you miss chances to turn the dial\n  toward a more rigorous form of questioning by accepting an immediate correct\n  answer or re-teaching a skill the student has already mastered.\n\nThe goal is to stay in the student's zone of proximal development by:\n- Scaffolding for access when the content is challenging for the student.\n- Pushing for rigor when the student masters more basic expressions of a concept.\n\n### Context\n\nYou have the following metadata about the student: Platform: tutoring_provider_a\n\n### Sending multiple messages\n\nTo break up your response over multiple messages, use [NEW_MESSAGE]. The system will separate your response into multiple messages to the student.\n\nHere is the conversation so far:\n\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification appears at the top center of the screen: \"Victoria has joined\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 2 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A video feed tile for the tutor appears on the left side of the screen. It is a red circle with a white \"V\" in the center. The name \"Victoria\" is displayed below the tile.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 291 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 231 seconds] The tutor is alone in the main room, waiting for the student to join.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification appears at the top of the screen: \"Kayla has joined\". A second participant tile appears for the student.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 87 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 1 second]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification appears at the top of the screen: \"Kayla has left\". The student's video feed disappears.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 31 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: Hi Kayla.\nTurn 2. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 5 seconds]\nTurn 2. STUDENT: Hi.\nTurn 3. TUTOR: How are you?\nTurn 4. STUDENT: Good. And you?\nTurn 5. TUTOR: I'm good. How's school been? Your first, what, three days?\nTurn 6. STUDENT: Um, it's been good.\nTurn 7. TUTOR: Are you liking your school so far?\nTurn 8. STUDENT: Um, yeah.\nTurn 9. TUTOR: Or is it just like, all right?\nTurn 10. STUDENT: I like it.\nTurn 11. TUTOR: Oh, that's good.\nTurn 12. TUTOR: Well, we can get started right away. Um, so, you're going to do, remember we're going to be practicing on your quick, uh, what's it called? Your multiplication, division, just like solving them quick at the end.\nTurn 13. TUTOR: Um, first, I'm going to show you 15 problems. They're pretty easy, okay? Like, they're, you should get most of them done. And what I'm going to do is I'm going to put on a 20-minute timer and see how many you can get done.\nTurn 14. TUTOR: The minimum you should get done is 10 questions, okay?\nTurn 15. STUDENT: Okay.\nTurn 16. TUTOR: So as soon as, um, you see them on your screen, get started right away, okay? And remember, you can always ask questions too.\nTurn 17. STUDENT: Okay.\nTurn 18. TUTOR: All right, here they are.\nTurn 19. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The tutor's video feed is replaced by a black screen.\nTurn 19. TUTOR: I'll check back in at 3:25.\nTurn 20. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 17 seconds]\nTurn 20. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1-15 - Math Problems (start)] A list of 15 math problems appears on the left side of the screen. The problems are:\n1. What is 6 x 4?\n2. Multiply: 12 x 3\n3. Add: 7 + 82\n4. What is the value of x if x + 8 = 12?\n5. What is the perimeter of a rectangle with length 8 cm and width 3 cm?\n6. Add: 1/2 + 1/2\n7. What is 9 x 2?\n8. Divide: 24 / 6\n9. What is 3/3 equal to?\n10. What is the value of x if 2x = 10?\n11. Subtract: 15 - 9\n12. What is the perimeter of a square with sides of 6 inches?\n13. Multiply: -8 x 7\n14. What is the value of x if x - 3 = 4?\n15. Add: 1M + 1M\nTutor's cursor moves to the right of the problem list, and a name tag \"Kayla\" appears.\nTurn 20. TUTOR: You have about 10, 12 minutes left, all right? Just a reminder.\nTurn 21. STUDENT: Okay.\nTurn 22. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 3 seconds]\nTurn 22. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - moves cursor] Tutor's cursor moves away, and the \"Kayla\" name tag disappears.\nTurn 22. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 356 seconds]\nTurn 22. TUTOR: Hi Kayla. You still have some time, but I was wondering which question are you on?\nTurn 23. STUDENT: I'm on eight.\nTurn 24. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - pointing] The tutor's cursor moves to point at question 8.\nTurn 24. TUTOR: All right. All right. Remember, try to go as fast as you can. Um, you should, I think you should be a little farther than eight, but just keep going. Also for number 10, um, go ahead and skip number 10, okay?\nTurn 25. STUDENT: Okay.\nTurn 26. TUTOR: Just keep going with the rest. Keep going, real fast.\nTurn 27. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 3 seconds]\nTurn 27. STUDENT: I need help on nine.\nTurn 28. TUTOR: Um, you can go ahead and skip nine for now too.\nTurn 29. STUDENT: Oh, okay.\nTurn 30. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 217 seconds]\nTurn 30. STUDENT: I'm confused on fourteen.\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nProvide targeted scaffolding now. Identify the specific unresolved obstacle in the student's current mathematical work. Offer only the smallest useful hint, representation, or clarification that makes the next step accessible. Use the learner's actual numbers and ideas when available. Leave the key calculation or inference for the student and invite one manageable next step. Do not give a full solution, stack several leading questions, repeat an explanation the learner already understands, or invent unseen worksheet details.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified policy in context. Give the student one clear, manageable invitation to think. Be mathematically accurate and concise. Do not mention policy names, routing, evaluation, or these instructions.\n/no_think"
